S={}
def add(slug,refs,text): S[slug]={'refs':refs,'miniText':text}
add('simple-birth-rates', [('wright','Provides the classical finite-population selection framework for comparing unequal reproductive rates.'),('moran','Separates random loss and fixation from a deterministic reproductive advantage; its overlapping-generation process differs from this model.'),('fisher','Frames differential reproduction as selection rather than purposeful adaptation.')], '''How does a small fertility difference change the composition of a bounded population? NetLogo Simple Birth Rates starts with red and blue agents, lets them reproduce at different rates, and randomly removes excess individuals above carrying capacity. The experiment illustrates selection through differential reproduction [1,3] while retaining chance extinction in a finite population [2]. Colors represent inherited types rather than explicit diploid genes, and the carrying-capacity correction gives every individual the same mortality risk. Consequently, dominance in one run does not establish a deterministic outcome or a universal selection coefficient. The simulation is an illustrative comparison of reproduction and sampling, not an empirically calibrated demographic forecast.''')
add('daisyworld',[('daisy','The original Daisyworld paper supplies the albedo–temperature–growth feedback motivating this NetLogo model.'),('daisyreview','Reviews Daisyworld variants and the limits of interpreting self-regulation as a general ecological result.'),('spatialdaisy','Addresses spatial environmental feedback, useful for distinguishing local grid interactions from a well-mixed original.')],'''Can organisms regulate an environment without intending to do so? NetLogo Daisyworld couples black and white daisies to temperature: their different albedos alter local heating, while temperature changes their ability to reproduce. Watson and Lovelock provide the original feedback argument [1]; subsequent analyses place its assumptions and spatial extensions in context [2,3]. The served NetLogo version makes local growth, heat diffusion, and changing luminosity observable. Its two daisy types, stylized growth response, and simplified energy balance are deliberate reductions. A temperature plateau demonstrates a possible feedback mechanism under these rules, not evidence that Earth's climate necessarily self-regulates or that evolutionary processes always stabilize environmental conditions.''')
add('termites',[('resnick','The scientific monograph is direct StarLogo lineage for the pickup-and-deposit microworld.'),('sorting','Studies decentralized pickup/deposit clustering, closely matching the abstract sorting mechanism.'),('stigmergy','Explains how environmental modifications can coordinate construction without a central plan.')],'''How can random walkers organize scattered material into piles? NetLogo Termites uses agents that pick up encountered wood chips and deposit them beside other chips. Material already deposited changes what later walkers encounter, producing environmental feedback without a shared blueprint. Resnick documents the microworld lineage [1], while collective-sorting research [2] and stigmergy [3] explain its relevant mechanism. Chips remain removable, so piles can merge rather than becoming permanently protected clusters. The agents' rules are abstractions of transport and aggregation, not a detailed account of termite architecture or behavior. Pile formation illustrates decentralized coordination; its appearance alone does not validate a biological explanation or guarantee convergence within a specified time.''')
add('shepherds',[('sorting','Supports interpreting pickup and release near existing groups as decentralized clustering.'),('resnick','Provides the termite microworld lineage explicitly referenced by this model.'),('shepherd','A contrasting scientific shepherding model exposes the difference between pickup-based aggregation and realistic repulsion-based herding.')],'''Which local transport rules can consolidate moving objects into a group? NetLogo Shepherds extends a termite-style clustering idea: wandering shepherds pick up sheep and release them near other sheep, while the sheep themselves also move. Collective sorting [1] and the StarLogo microworld lineage [2] provide the closest interpretation of these rules. A separate shepherding study [3] offers a useful comparison because actual herding need not involve carrying animals. Relative movement speeds and agent numbers can change whether aggregation persists. This is therefore an abstract experiment in clustering mobile objects, not a validated description of livestock management. A visible herd is evidence of the stated algorithm's behavior under chosen settings only.''')
add('rabbits-grass-weeds',[('rosenzweig','Frames feedback between consumer abundance and renewable food availability.'),('tilman','Explains why food growth and resource competition matter for coexistence of grass and weeds.'),('holling','Highlights how assumptions about encounter and feeding rates affect consumer-resource outcomes.')],'''How do food quality and regrowth affect a population of consumers? NetLogo Rabbits Grass Weeds lets wandering rabbits eat two plant resources, gain energy, reproduce, and die when energy is exhausted. Grass and weeds can differ in growth and energetic value. Consumer-resource feedback [1], resource competition [2], and feeding-response theory [3] frame the scientific questions this microworld illustrates. Its random movement and patch-level eating rules replace many biological details, including explicit plant physiology, age structure, and measured feeding responses. Changes in population trajectories show consequences of the declared resource and energy assumptions. They should not be read as calibrated predictions of rabbit carrying capacity, agricultural yield, or stable coexistence in a particular habitat.''')
add('invasive-plants',[('auld','The served Info explicitly names this spreading-plant model as a loose inspiration.'),('skellam','Provides foundational dispersal theory for relating local spread to population expansion.'),('invasions','Reviews mechanisms and limitations in spatial invasion predictions and management.')],'''How do local spread and treatment interact across neighboring farms? NetLogo Invasive Plants assigns each patch an infestation level, permits spread, and applies weed-killer treatment with effects extending to nearby patches. The Info identifies Auld and Coote as a loose source [1]; dispersal theory [2] and invasion research [3] provide broader scientific context. The model makes spatial externalities and repeated intervention visible without representing individual seeds, crop economics, or chemical transport in detail. Its bounded infestation scale is an abstraction rather than a measured density. Comparing interventions illustrates consequences of the chosen spread and treatment rules, not demonstrated field effectiveness, pesticide safety, or a forecast for a named invasive species.''')
add('ants',[('goss','Shows how reinforcing chemical trails can select shorter routes without global optimization.'),('antforage','Studies self-organized exploratory paths in ants, directly relevant to foraging and recruitment.'),('anttrail','Connects trail deposition and path choice to experimentally studied ant behavior.')],'''Can a colony exploit food sources using only local chemical information? NetLogo Ants combines wandering search with pheromone deposition by ants carrying food home. Other ants follow the chemical field, while diffusion and evaporation continually modify it. Research on ant shortcuts [1], exploratory patterns [2], and trail-dependent path choice [3] provides mechanism-specific context. The served model emphasizes reinforcement and depletion rather than individual route maps or centralized allocation. Its chemical grid, movement rules, and simplified food piles omit species-specific sensing and recruitment details. Efficient-looking paths illustrate a possible collective effect of local feedback. They do not prove global optimality or establish that the model quantitatively reproduces a real colony.''')
add('heatbugs',[('swarm','Documents the Swarm platform named in the model lineage; supports interpretation as an artificial-agent demonstration.'),('repast','Documents another platform explicitly named by NetLogo Heatbugs and the importance of implementation semantics.'),('align','Addresses alignment between independently implemented agent models, directly relevant to the Info caveat about untested cross-toolkit equivalence.')],'''How can individual temperature preferences generate collective spatial patterns? NetLogo Heatbugs places heat-producing agents on a grid with heat diffusion and cooling. Uncomfortable bugs seek warmer or cooler unoccupied neighboring cells, with occasional random movement. The scientific context is an artificial-agent platform demonstration [1,2] and the problem of aligning implementations [3], rather than a species-specific biological theory. The Info explicitly says that rigorous equivalence with Swarm and Repast was not established. Heterogeneous preferences, occupancy constraints, and field dynamics can produce clusters or avoidance. These are outcomes of the specified artificial rules; they do not establish biological thermoregulation, physical calibration of heat units, or verified equivalence among toolkit versions.''')
add('flocking',[('reynolds','Introduces the separation, alignment and cohesion mechanism underlying the model lineage.'),('vicsek','Offers a minimal local-alignment model for comparison with multi-rule flocking.'),('ballerini','Empirical flock research tests assumptions about which neighbors determine collective motion.')],'''How can coordinated motion arise without a leader? NetLogo Flocking applies separation, alignment, and cohesion using locally visible neighbors. These rules correspond to the distributed flocking tradition introduced by Reynolds [1], while minimal alignment models [2] and empirical work on neighbor selection [3] clarify which assumptions matter. The simulation illustrates moving groups that form and reorganize through repeated local updates. Its bounded turns, chosen vision distance, and stylized motion do not explicitly model flight energetics, sensory uncertainty, or aerodynamic interactions. A coherent flock demonstrates an emergent property of these rules. It does not establish that a particular bird species uses the same metric neighborhood or that every parameter setting maintains cohesion.''')
add('fireflies',[('buck','Reviews observed synchronous firefly flashing and its biological diversity.'),('carlson','Provides biological context for flash communication rather than treating all flashes as generic oscillations.'),('mirollo','Supplies a mathematical pulse-coupled synchronization comparison with explicit assumptions.')],'''When can locally interacting flashers synchronize their rhythms? NetLogo Fireflies represents individuals with internal flash timing and rules that advance or delay their cycle after observing neighbors. Biological accounts of synchronized flashing [1] and communication [2] motivate the example, while pulse-coupled oscillator theory [3] supplies a mathematical comparison. The simulation makes the effect of population density, timing rules, and interaction range visible. Its discrete counters and simplified perception omit species-specific signal recognition, sex differences, and measured response curves. Synchrony in a run illustrates coordination without a central clock. It does not imply that every firefly species synchronizes or that the assumptions of a continuous oscillator theorem hold exactly in this NetLogo implementation.''')
add('chloroplasts-and-food',[('calvin','Provides the carbon-fixation pathway context for the model’s simplified sugar production.'),('farquhar','A quantitative photosynthesis model clarifies what is omitted by a qualitative molecular animation.'),('hill','Original isolated-chloroplast oxygen research grounds the distinction between light-driven reactions and carbon assimilation.')],'''How do light-dependent energy transfer and carbon fixation connect in photosynthesis? NetLogo Chloroplasts and Food depicts a single plant cell, with simplified chloroplast processes that connect water, carbon dioxide, energy carriers, and sugar formation. Carbon-fixation research [1], quantitative assimilation modeling [2], and chloroplast oxygen experiments [3] place these representations in scientific context. The served model is a curricular sequence of interacting components, not a full biochemical reconstruction of the Calvin cycle or electron transport. Molecule counts and encounter rules are teaching abstractions rather than measured concentrations or rate constants. It illustrates dependencies among inputs and outputs; visual agreement with a pathway diagram does not establish physiological accuracy or predict plant productivity.''')
add('slime',[('keller','The Info explicitly identifies the Keller–Segel aggregation instability as scientific origin.'),('bonner','Original chemotaxis work supplies empirical context for collective aggregation.'),('kessin','A specialist scientific monograph explains biological development beyond the simplified chemical-attraction model.')],'''Can chemical attraction produce aggregation without a pacemaker cell? NetLogo Slime lets mobile cells respond to a chemical field that they also help create. Local reinforcement can turn an initially dispersed population into clusters. Keller and Segel's instability framework [1] is explicitly named in the Info; experimental chemotaxis [2] and Dictyostelium biology [3] contextualize the mechanism. The model reduces development to movement, signaling, diffusion, and signal loss, without reconstructing complete multicellular differentiation or a measured biochemical network. Its clusters illustrate a possible decentralized aggregation mechanism. They do not demonstrate that all slime molds share this life cycle, or that a successful run quantitatively matches an organism under laboratory conditions.''')
add('fur',[('turing','Foundational diffusion-driven instability theory supplies the broad pattern-formation context.'),('young','The local activator–inhibitor rule is the specific scientific variant named in the Info.'),('economou','An experimental mammalian patterning study gives a relevant test of a Turing mechanism without validating this fur implementation.')],'''Can short-range activation and longer-range inhibition generate different skin-like patterns? NetLogo Fur uses a lattice of differentiated and undifferentiated pigment cells. Weighted contributions from inner and outer neighborhoods determine whether cells change state. Turing supplies the general pattern-forming idea [1], and Young supplies the local-rule variant identified by the Info [2]. Experimental mammalian patterning research [3] illustrates how related mechanisms can be tested biologically. The simulation assumes fixed neighborhood geometry and reduced cell states, without explicit molecular concentrations, tissue growth, or species-specific genetics. Spots and stripes demonstrate the repertoire of this rule system. Their resemblance to real fur is not empirical validation of the implementation or proof of a universal pigmentation mechanism.''')
add('blood-sugar-regulation',[('bergman','Provides quantitative glucose–insulin regulation context and a contrast to qualitative particle encounters.'),('unger','Establishes glucagon’s counter-regulatory role relevant to liver–pancreas feedback.'),('rorsman','Explains beta-cell insulin secretion mechanisms that the model deliberately compresses.')],'''How can interacting organs stabilize blood glucose after disturbances? NetLogo Blood Sugar Regulation represents liver and pancreatic tissue as patches and glucose, insulin, and glucagon as moving molecules. Local interactions implement opposing hormonal responses rather than explicit blood vessels. Quantitative glucose regulation [1], glucagon physiology [2], and beta-cell secretion research [3] support the model's scientific framing. Its particle diffusion and simplified cell rules compress circulation, receptor signaling, and metabolic pathways into visible events. The simulation illustrates negative feedback and the distinction between increasing and decreasing glucose availability. It is not a calibrated physiological model, cannot determine a person's insulin sensitivity, and should not be interpreted as evidence for a clinical intervention or dosing decision.''')
add('honeycomb',[('camazine','Examines self-organized comb patterning, providing biological context for emergent organization.'),('karihaloo','Studies a physical mechanism of circular-to-hexagonal cell transformation, an alternative to this model’s pheromone rules.'),('hales','Establishes the mathematical honeycomb problem, distinguishing geometric efficiency from a construction mechanism.')],'''Can local movement feedback generate a honeycomb-like lattice? NetLogo Honeycomb has bees move in circles while leaving a diffusing, evaporating chemical; the chemical changes movement speed and turning radius. Its Info attributes these artificial rules to David Little and explicitly avoids claiming that real bees follow them. Scientific studies of comb organization [1] and physical cell transformation [2] provide biological comparisons, while the honeycomb theorem [3] addresses geometric efficiency. These are distinct explanations and should not be conflated. The simulation illustrates regular pattern formation from a chosen feedback loop. Hexagonal-looking output neither identifies the actual construction behavior of bees nor proves that this chemical mechanism optimizes real wax use.''')
add('autumn',[('hoch','Investigates the physiological significance of anthocyanins during leaf senescence.'),('matile','Explains pigment and senescence processes behind autumn coloration.'),('lee','Provides scientific context for anthocyanins beyond a single visible color variable.')],'''How do changing environmental conditions produce visible autumn leaf colors? NetLogo Autumn represents the production and loss of pigments, then connects their relative presence to leaf appearance and falling. Research on senescent-leaf anthocyanins [1], seasonal pigment biochemistry [2], and vegetative anthocyanins [3] supplies the relevant biological context. The served model combines effects of sunlight, temperature, and water through simplified rules. Its colors summarize underlying chemistry rather than resolve optical spectra, species-specific pigment pathways, or measured weather responses. Varying the conditions illustrates how several processes can contribute to a seasonal pattern. A plausible display is not validation of pigment concentrations, an explanation of every species' autumn colors, or a forecast of the timing of leaf fall.''')
add('membrane-formation',[('gazzola','The Info cites this self-assembled-structure research as part of the model’s scientific lineage.'),('bedau','The Info cites the DDPD ligation-model work underlying this artificial-chemistry approach.'),('israelachvili','Provides physical self-assembly theory for comparing simplified hydrophilic and hydrophobic interactions with real amphiphiles.')],'''How can simple interactions between water and amphiphilic molecules create organized boundaries? NetLogo Membrane Formation represents lipids with connected hydrophilic and hydrophobic parts, plus water particles. Attraction and repulsion can produce collective structures without an imposed membrane template. The Info connects this approach to artificial-chemistry research [1,2]; amphiphile self-assembly theory [3] supplies a physical comparison. The interaction selection and movement rules are computational approximations rather than a molecular force field with calibrated units. Resulting aggregates illustrate how local preferences can organize space. Their appearance alone does not establish thermodynamic equilibrium, realistic bilayer elasticity, or a complete account of biological membrane formation, which also depends on composition and environmental conditions.''')
add('sunflower-emergent',[('douady','Provides the physical self-organized phyllotaxis precedent named in the Info.'),('vogel','A mathematical sunflower construction gives a geometric comparison to repulsive-energy placement.'),('auxin','A biological phyllotaxis model highlights developmental mechanisms omitted by the purely physical illustration.')],'''Can a spiral arrangement emerge without prescribing the golden angle? NetLogo Sunflower Emergent adds particles on successive rings and selects positions by minimizing an approximation of repulsive energy. The Info describes a Stenger-inspired construction and relates it to physical phyllotaxis [1]. A geometric sunflower model [2] and a developmental model [3] offer useful comparisons. The simulation treats growth as sequential point placement with chosen energy rules; it does not represent a complete plant meristem or auxin transport. Double spirals illustrate the consequences of spatial exclusion and growth under these assumptions. They do not establish that repulsion alone explains every plant pattern or that a matching image confirms a biological mechanism.''')
add('tumor',[('bonnet','Original leukemia hierarchy research gives empirical context for distinguishing stem-like and more limited progenitor populations.'),('reya','Explains the cancer stem-cell framework relevant to persistent self-renewing cells.'),('enderling','Shows why spatial growth constraints matter when interpreting cell-based tumor simulations.')],'''How can cell hierarchies affect tumor persistence after treatment? NetLogo Tumor distinguishes self-renewing stem cells from transitory cells with limited proliferation and eventual death. Its rules include asymmetric or symmetric division and a stylized secondary colony. Cancer hierarchy research [1,2] frames the contrast between persistent and short-lived populations, while spatial tumor modeling [3] highlights constraints on growth. The Info credits an original StarLogoT contribution by Gershom Zajicek. This is a qualitative demonstration with selected cell types and simplified movement, aging, and treatment rules. It illustrates how targeting one compartment can leave another intact, but does not validate clinical resistance mechanisms, estimate a patient's prognosis, or recommend cancer therapy.''')
add('muscle-development',[('schoenfeld','Reviews mechanisms of exercise-induced hypertrophy, useful for interpreting the model’s growth abstraction.'),('phillips','Analyzes resistance training and muscle mass with more physiological detail than the model.'),('hormone','Tests and challenges a simple causal link from transient anabolic-hormone elevations to muscle protein synthesis.')],'''How do training frequency and recovery assumptions change simulated muscle development? NetLogo Muscle Development links weight lifting, sleep, and simplified hormonal balance to changes in muscle. Hypertrophy mechanisms [1] and research on resistance training [2] provide scientific context. Evidence questioning a simple link between transient circulating hormones and protein synthesis [3] is particularly important when interpreting the model's explanatory story. Its hormone and recovery variables compress many biological pathways into illustrative rules and are not personalized physiological measurements. The simulation can expose interactions and delayed consequences within those rules. It does not establish an optimal training plan or demonstrate that changing one modeled hormone directly produces the depicted amount of muscle growth.''')
add('mimicry',[('bates','The original Amazonian butterfly study introduces the mimicry tradition motivating the model.'),('brower','An experimental monarch–viceroy mimicry study directly addresses the predator-learning example.'),('ritland','Challenges the assumption that the viceroy is palatable, a limitation explicitly acknowledged in the served Info.')],'''How can predator learning favor resemblance between defended and undefended prey? NetLogo Mimicry represents birds that remember unpleasant prey colors and butterflies that reproduce with occasional color changes. Avoidance can favor similar appearances without deliberate imitation. Bates's original work [1] and experiments on monarch–viceroy mimicry [2] provide historical scientific context. Later evidence that viceroys are themselves unpalatable [3] challenges the particular example, as the Info acknowledges. The model assumes a simplified memory capacity, color space, and reproductive process. It illustrates selection mediated by learning, not a verified reconstruction of monarch–viceroy evolution. Neither matching colors nor predator avoidance establishes that the modeled species relationship is genuinely Batesian.''')
add('anisogamy',[('parker','Foundational gamete-dimorphism theory supplies the size–number tradeoff and disruptive-selection context.'),('anisogamymodel','The publication is directly cited by the served model as its multi-agent thought-experiment lineage.'),('bulmer','A game-theoretic development examines the assumptions underlying anisogamy evolution.')],'''Under what assumptions can two gamete sizes evolve from an initially less differentiated population? NetLogo Anisogamy explores reproductive investment, gamete encounters, and offspring survival through spatially explicit agents. Foundational gamete-dimorphism theory [1], the model authors' thought-experiment study [2], and a game-theoretic analysis [3] frame the evolutionary question. The Info presents the model as a tool for challenging and comparing explanations, rather than a universal origin story. Discrete time, spatial encounters, and the specified relation between gamete investment and success constrain interpretation. Emerging size differences illustrate one set of evolutionary consequences. They do not establish that every biological lineage followed the same path or that the chosen survival and encounter rules are empirically calibrated.''')
add('peppered-moths',[('kettlewell','Original selection experiments ground the industrial-melanism example.'),('cook','Later field predation evidence tests the selective mechanism under improved conditions.'),('mothgene','Identifies a molecular basis of British peppered-moth melanism beyond the model’s simplified color inheritance.')],'''How can environmental change reverse selection on a visible trait? NetLogo Peppered Moths gives moth colors different predation risks against changing backgrounds and follows their frequencies across reproduction. Kettlewell's experiments [1] establish the historical case; later predation work [2] and molecular research [3] strengthen and refine its interpretation. The served model represents predation invisibly and simplifies inheritance, pollution, and habitat differences. It illustrates how differential survival can change population composition when camouflage conditions change. Its outputs do not reproduce a historical time series without calibration, and a background-color rule cannot substitute for field evidence about resting behavior, predator choice, or the genetic mechanism that produces the actual moth phenotypes.''')
add('baldwin-effect',[('baldwin','Original organic-selection proposal establishes the historical learning–evolution question.'),('hinton','The computational learning-guided evolution paper supplies the central model lineage.'),('maynard','A contemporary analysis interprets how learning can alter evolutionary search without inheritance of acquired traits.')],'''Can individual learning change the course of genetic evolution without acquired traits being inherited? NetLogo Baldwin Effect explores how learning helps organisms find successful behavior while reproduction changes a population's genetic composition. Baldwin's proposal [1], Hinton and Nowlan's computational study [2], and Maynard Smith's analysis [3] frame this question. The served Info identifies the Hinton–Nowlan approach as its starting point, with explicit extensions and parameter choices. Learning effort, genotype representation, and the chosen fitness environment are model assumptions rather than measured universal properties. Successful runs illustrate interaction between plasticity and selection. They do not imply Lamarckian inheritance, prove that learning always speeds evolution, or establish the effect's magnitude in a particular natural population.''')
add('mammoths',[('alroy','A multispecies overkill simulation directly represents the hunting explanation being contrasted.'),('cooper','Abrupt warming and megafaunal turnover provide empirical context for climate-related explanations.'),('nogues','Joint climate-and-human modeling shows why single-cause interpretations require caution.')],'''How can hunting pressure and environmental deterioration contribute to extinction? NetLogo Mammoths contrasts simplified hunting and climate-change explanations for megafaunal loss. An overkill simulation [1], evidence relating warming to population turnover [2], and research combining climate and human influences [3] provide complementary scientific contexts. The model uses stylized populations and environmental rules to make possible causal pathways visible. Its historical dates and narrative should be checked against the publications rather than treated as current chronological consensus. Extinction in a run demonstrates a consequence of chosen assumptions, not a settled attribution of real mammoth disappearance. Parameter sensitivity and the interaction of pressures matter, and the simulation alone cannot distinguish among competing historical explanations.''')
add('sex-ratio-equilibrium',[('fisher','Supplies the frequency-dependent parental-investment argument named by the model.'),('hamilton','Explores conditions yielding extraordinary sex ratios, exposing limits of a universal 1:1 expectation.'),('charnov','A specialist theory of sex allocation contextualizes investment, inheritance and mating assumptions.')],'''Why can frequency-dependent selection favor a balanced sex ratio? NetLogo Sex Ratio Equilibrium follows inherited reproductive tendencies under biparental reproduction and Mendelian assumptions. Fisher's argument [1] supplies the central intuition: the rarer sex can have a reproductive advantage. Hamilton's analysis [2] and sex-allocation theory [3] identify conditions under which ratios can differ. The served model investigates an adult demographic ratio with particular inheritance and reproductive rules; adult ratios must not automatically be equated with parental investment at conception. Equilibrium-like output illustrates those assumptions, not a biological law requiring every population to contain equal numbers of males and females. Differential survival, mating structure, and unequal reproductive costs can change the interpretation.''')
add('bacterial-infection',[('nash','Outer-membrane permeability research relates directly to the model’s antibiotic-entry abstraction.'),('levin','Population-genetic analysis explains how antibiotic exposure changes resistant-type frequencies.'),('andersson','Reviews resistance costs and reversibility, highlighting mechanisms omitted by a simple porosity trait.')],'''How does antibiotic exposure change the composition of a bacterial population? NetLogo Bacterial Infection represents bacteria with different membrane porosities, reproduction when space is available, and antibiotics entering through membrane openings. Permeability research [1], resistance population genetics [2], and evidence on resistance costs [3] provide the scientific context. The served curricular model compares stylized treatment environments and makes differential survival visible. Its pores are an explicit teaching abstraction, not a complete representation of resistance mechanisms, pharmacokinetics, or mutation pathways. A population becoming harder to eliminate illustrates selection under those rules. It does not validate a clinical treatment schedule, establish that all resistance works through porosity, or quantify outcomes for an individual patient.''')
add('vision-evolution',[('maciver','The model explicitly derives its motivating eye-size and water-to-land hypothesis from this paper.'),('nilsson','A quantitative eye-evolution thought experiment provides a relevant comparison for gradual changes in vision.'),('land','Specialist visual-system research emphasizes structural and optical constraints absent from the simplified perception radius.')],'''Can changes in visual opportunity interact with a transition between water and land? NetLogo Vision Evolution represents vertebrate-like agents whose eye size affects perception across two environments, alongside changes relevant to terrestrial movement. The motivating hypothesis is explicitly attributed to MacIver and colleagues [1]. Eye-evolution modeling [2] and research on animal visual systems [3] provide comparisons for its assumptions. The simulation reduces optics, morphology, food detection, and inheritance to selected traits and rules. It illustrates how a hypothesized ecological opportunity could alter selection within that constructed setting. It does not reconstruct a fossil lineage, establish a unique causal sequence for terrestrial evolution, or show that larger eyes alone caused the origin of limbs.''')
add('gendrift-t-interact',[('moran','Provides a finite-population neutral-drift framework for interpreting chance fixation.'),('clifford','A spatial copying/conflict process is mathematically close to neighboring agents adopting each other’s states.'),('kimura','Distinguishes neutral change from adaptive selection, relevant to the absence of color fitness differences.')],'''Can a population lose diversity without any color being advantageous? NetLogo GenDrift T Interact starts moving agents with several colors; neighboring encounters cause one agent to copy another's color. Colors that disappear cannot return. Finite-population drift [1], spatial copying processes [2], and neutral evolutionary theory [3] frame the resulting fixation and regional differences. The model's walls can isolate groups, altering which agents can interact. Color copying stands in for inherited variation rather than explicitly modeling mating, chromosomes, or reproduction. Consensus in a connected run illustrates stochastic loss under the specified interaction rules. It does not identify an adaptive benefit, establish that every spatial arrangement reaches global consensus, or reproduce a calibrated genetic history.''')
add('mendelian-inheritance',[('mendel','A scholarly translation of Mendel’s original paper supplies the segregation foundation, with translators credited as published.'),('castle','A scientific monograph on mouse coat-color genetics gives organism-specific context and exposes multi-locus complexity.'),('bultman','Molecular analysis of the mouse agouti locus provides a concrete example beyond the model’s one-trait abstraction.')],'''How do parental genotypes produce predictable offspring ratios while individual births remain uncertain? NetLogo Mendelian Inheritance lets users breed a male and female mouse with homozygous or heterozygous states and observe offspring coat colors. Mendel's experiments, available in a scholarly translation [1], ground segregation; mouse coat-color genetics [2] and molecular agouti research [3] show the biological detail behind the example. The simulation reduces inheritance to the chosen dominant–recessive trait and simplified reproduction. Expected ratios describe repeated sampling, not a requirement that every small litter has exact proportions. The model illustrates allele transmission and genotype–phenotype distinctions, while omitting much of the multi-locus regulation and developmental biology underlying real coat colors.''')
add('disease-solo',[('kermack','Foundational epidemic dynamics contextualize transmission through susceptible contacts.'),('funk','Behavioral awareness research is relevant to the model’s avoid/chase strategies and user-controlled movement.'),('ferguson','Intervention modeling demonstrates why movement and contact assumptions matter when interpreting reduced epidemic experiments.')],'''How does movement behavior alter opportunities for infectious contact? NetLogo Disease Solo combines one user-controlled person with computer-controlled agents, and infection spreads through encounters. Options for avoidance and pursuit let users explore how behavior changes exposure. Classical epidemic dynamics [1], awareness–disease feedback [2], and intervention modeling [3] provide scientific context. This is a one-player derivative of the HubNet Disease activity, not a fitted surveillance model. Its android strategies and simplified infection rule deliberately isolate contact effects without reconstructing an actual population's social network or pathogen biology. Different outbreak patterns illustrate consequences of movement choices within the simulation. They do not establish real intervention effectiveness or predict infection risk for an individual.''')
add('repressilator-1d',[('elowitz','The original synthetic repressilator is the explicit scientific reference in the model Info.'),('gillespie','Provides the stochastic reaction-simulation framework for comparing random molecular events with formal chemical kinetics.'),('potvin','Later synthetic-circuit research tests and improves oscillator reliability beyond the original demonstration.')],'''How can a ring of inhibitory interactions generate repeated changes in gene expression? NetLogo Repressilator 1D represents DNA, mRNA, and regulatory proteins in a simplified three-component repression cycle. The original synthetic oscillator [1] supplies the direct scientific inspiration. Stochastic reaction methods [2] and later robust oscillator experiments [3] clarify the role of molecular noise and circuit design. The animation uses one-dimensional movement and selected production, binding, and degradation rules; it should not automatically be treated as an exact chemical master-equation implementation. Oscillations illustrate feedback with delay under these assumptions. They do not prove quantitative agreement with the original biological construct, establish parameter robustness, or reproduce every measured cellular time scale.''')
add('hiv',[('andersonhiv','Relates sexual contact patterns to HIV population dynamics, directly relevant to coupling assumptions.'),('weller','Systematically assesses condom effectiveness, grounding one modeled prevention mechanism.'),('cohen','Early-treatment evidence highlights a major modern prevention pathway omitted by the simplified model.')],'''How do partnership duration, testing, and safer-sex assumptions change transmission opportunities? NetLogo HIV forms and dissolves couples in an isolated population and distinguishes unknown from known infection. The served model assumes that people who know they are infected consistently practice safer sex. Research on sexual-contact structure [1], condom effectiveness [2], and early antiretroviral treatment [3] frames both its mechanisms and omissions. Testing behavior is represented through simple tendencies, and the model does not reconstruct contemporary treatment access, adherence, or all transmission routes. It illustrates how local partnerships and information can affect population patterns. It does not provide a calibrated estimate of HIV risk or demonstrate the effectiveness of an actual public-health program.''')
add('virus',[('yorke','The model explicitly cites this study of viral persistence, seasonality and population requirements.'),('kermack','Provides the susceptible–infectious–removed baseline against which demographic replenishment and immunity changes can be compared.'),('bartlett','Empirical epidemic periodicity and community-size research contextualizes persistence and stochastic fadeout.')],'''What allows an infection to persist when susceptibility, immunity, births, and deaths interact? NetLogo Virus moves people among susceptible, infectious, and immune states, with demographic renewal and disease-related mortality. The Info explicitly cites research on viral perpetuation [1]; classical epidemic theory [2] and community-size evidence [3] provide further context. Contact emerges from random movement rather than a measured social network, and immunity duration and recovery are controlled by stylized parameters. The model illustrates how replenishment of susceptible individuals can change outbreak trajectories compared with a closed epidemic. Persistence or extinction in one run does not establish a population threshold for a real virus, empirical calibration, or a reliable forecast of future outbreaks.''')
add('crispr-bacterium',[('barrangou','Original experimental evidence connects acquired CRISPR sequences to antiviral resistance.'),('brouns','Identifies small CRISPR RNAs in antiviral defense, directly supporting sequence-specific recognition.'),('moddell','Studies acquisition during viral DNA injection, relevant to extending the model’s memory-formation mechanism.')],'''How can a bacterium acquire sequence-specific memory of viral exposure? NetLogo CRISPR Bacterium follows a single cell confronted by viruses and represents recognition through stored sequence information. Original resistance experiments [1], small-RNA defense research [2], and work on viral-DNA-driven acquisition [3] supply direct scientific context. The served model distinguishes adaptive from innate protection while compressing molecular machinery into visible events. It omits many biochemical constraints, diverse CRISPR–Cas architectures, and the environmental variation surrounding real cells. Survival after previous exposure illustrates the logic of acquired specificity under the chosen rules. It does not establish a measured immune efficiency, reproduce the complete molecular pathway, or imply that all bacteria possess the same defense system.''')
add('crispr-ecosystem',[('iranzo','An explicit ecological CRISPR–Cas model directly addresses the population-level feedback represented here.'),('paez','Empirical phage-genome evolution under CRISPR pressure grounds the coevolution question.'),('sun','Studies phage mutations during bacterial CRISPR diversification, supporting a dynamic rather than static immunity interpretation.')],'''How does adaptive bacterial immunity reshape competition between bacteria and viruses? NetLogo CRISPR Ecosystem models interacting populations with innate and CRISPR-mediated protection, allowing comparison of ecological and evolutionary consequences. Ecological CRISPR theory [1] and empirical studies of phage evolution [2,3] provide mechanism-specific context. The served model describes a tiny seawater-like setting, but uses simplified encounters, growth, resistance, and viral change rather than a calibrated microbial community. The simulation illustrates feedback between host defense and viral pressure, and how population outcomes differ when adaptive memory is available. It does not demonstrate a universal coexistence rule, quantify real marine abundances, or validate all of the molecular and ecological assumptions through visual agreement alone.''')
add('epidem-basic',[('kermack','The Info explicitly identifies the Kermack–McKendrick model as the mathematical basis.'),('bartlett','Community-size evidence emphasizes finite-population effects absent from deterministic compartment equations.'),('gillespie','Provides a formal stochastic-event comparison for interpreting agent-level randomness without asserting algorithmic equivalence.')],'''How does an initially rare infection spread through a susceptible population? NetLogo epiDEM Basic is explicitly based on the Kermack–McKendrick epidemic framework [1], but makes individual movement and infection events visible. Susceptible, infectious, and recovered states evolve in a closed population with no births, deaths, or travel. Community-size research [2] and stochastic reaction methods [3] help distinguish finite random realizations from deterministic aggregate equations. These references contextualize the model; they do not imply that its stepping algorithm is Gillespie's method. The simulation illustrates depletion of susceptible individuals and outbreak variability. Its stylized contacts, fixed biological assumptions, and uncalibrated parameters do not establish a forecast for any named disease or community.''')
add('epidem-travel-and-control',[('kermack','Supplies the epidemic baseline extended by movement and intervention rules.'),('hufnagel','Connects travel structure to epidemic propagation and containment.'),('ferguson','A scientific intervention-modeling study provides context for isolation, travel restrictions and vaccination assumptions.')],'''How do mobility and control measures change the spread of infection? NetLogo epiDEM Travel and Control extends the basic epidemic with travel, isolation, quarantine, inoculation, and individual links. Classical epidemic theory [1], transport-linked epidemic modeling [2], and intervention analysis [3] frame these mechanisms. The served Info assumes no viral mutation and perfect immunity after recovery, which limits the diseases and time scales the example can represent. Its contact and control rules are illustrative abstractions, not fitted estimates of compliance, vaccine protection, or real travel flows. Comparing scenarios exposes consequences of these assumptions. It does not establish policy effectiveness for an actual outbreak or replace empirical evaluation of interventions and uncertainty.''')
add('genevo-1-genetic-switch',[('jacob','The original protein-regulation paper supplies the lac-operon scientific foundation.'),('ozbudak','Experiments on lactose-network multistability contextualize switching and history dependence.'),('chung','Single-cell switching research shows stochastic molecular triggers beyond the model’s simplified encounter rules.')],'''How can the same cell switch gene expression in response to an environmental resource? NetLogo GenEvo 1 Genetic Switch represents the lac operon through DNA regions, regulatory proteins, RNA polymerase, lactose, and expressed enzymes. Jacob and Monod's regulatory framework [1], lactose-network multistability [2], and single-cell switching research [3] provide the scientific context. The model makes binding and transcription events visible while compressing molecular transport, concentrations, and reaction kinetics. It illustrates regulation through interactions rather than a prewritten instruction to turn a gene on. The resulting switch is a consequence of selected rules, not proof of quantitative agreement with every bacterial strain or experimental condition, nor a complete account of lac-operon regulation.''')
add('dna-protein-synthesis',[('crick','The central-dogma paper contextualizes directional information transfer without claiming every molecular detail is represented.'),('nirenberg','Original cell-free synthesis research grounds the connection between nucleotide sequences and amino-acid production.'),('frameshift','Original genetic-code work directly contextualizes insertion/deletion reading-frame effects.')],'''How do sequence changes alter the products of gene expression? NetLogo DNA Protein Synthesis depicts DNA, transcription into mRNA, and translation involving tRNA and amino-acid chains. Users can compare substitutions, insertions, and deletions and observe their modeled consequences. The central dogma [1], code-deciphering experiments [2], and genetic reading-frame work [3] provide scientific context. The served model is a curricular representation of selected molecular steps, not a complete simulation of folding, splicing, quality control, or cellular regulation. It illustrates why a sequence change can have different consequences depending on its position and type. Its animations do not establish actual protein function, the clinical significance of a variant, or quantitative synthesis rates.''')
add('ant-lines',[('bruckstein','The mathematical ant-trail paper is explicitly identified by the Info as the model’s inspiration.'),('pursuit','Cyclic-pursuit control theory provides a mathematical comparison for agents steering toward predecessors.'),('tandem','Experimental tandem-running research distinguishes real ant following and teaching from the geometric pursuit illustration.')],'''Why can a chain of followers trace a smoother route than its leader? NetLogo Ant Lines sends a leader toward food along a wandering path; each later ant heads toward the position of the ant ahead. Bruckstein's mathematical study [1] is the explicit model inspiration. Pursuit dynamics [2] and experimental tandem running [3] provide complementary comparisons. The served Info itself says biological accuracy is uncertain. Delays, turning, and repeated local following generate a geometric filtering effect without a chemical trail or global route plan. The simulation illustrates a mathematical mechanism for path smoothing, not a validated account of ant navigation, evidence of teaching by its agents, or a proof of optimal foraging routes.''')
add('beesmart-hive-finding',[('seeley','The scientific monograph is explicitly named as the model’s behavioral basis.'),('quorum','Tests quorum sensing during nest-site choice, directly relevant to the decision threshold.'),('stop','Examines cross-inhibition between scouts, useful for interpreting competition among candidate sites.')],'''How can scouts collectively choose a nest site without a leader comparing every option? NetLogo BeeSmart Hive Finding represents the actively searching minority of a honeybee swarm. Scouts discover sites, communicate support, and contribute to a collective decision through simplified behavioral rules. Seeley's synthesis [1], quorum-sensing experiments [2], and stop-signal research [3] supply the relevant scientific context. The Info explicitly notes modifications and the omission of most non-scout bees. Its spatial layout, quality assessments, and decision rules are abstractions rather than a complete reconstruction of a field swarm. A chosen site illustrates distributed evidence accumulation under those assumptions, not proof that the colony always finds the globally best option or that every signaling mechanism is represented.''')
add('moths',[('fabian','Recent experimental work directly challenges simple light-seeking or celestial-compass explanations and is cited in the served Info.'),('warrant','Explains constraints of nocturnal insect vision that the model simplifies.'),('frank','Reviews ecological impacts of artificial light, separating an illustrative flight mechanism from consequences for populations.')],'''Which local orientation rule could make insects circle a light? NetLogo Moths produces curved flight around a source by combining simple movement and orientation rules. The scientific question remains distinct from the visual resemblance: recent flight research [1], nocturnal-vision analysis [2], and light-pollution synthesis [3] constrain interpretation. The served Info explicitly discusses competing explanations and cites modern evidence. Its agents do not reconstruct full three-dimensional flight control, body orientation, or species-specific sensory processing. Circling therefore demonstrates what the implemented rule can produce. It does not establish that real insects navigate by that rule, resolve all attraction-to-light mechanisms, or quantify the ecological effects of a particular lighting installation.''')
add('flocking-vee-formations',[('nathan','The Info explicitly states that the artificial-bird rules are loosely based on this paper.'),('portugal','Empirical formation-flight research addresses aerodynamic benefits that the simple model does not calculate.'),('lissaman','Foundational formation-flight analysis provides the physical context for spacing and upwash assumptions.')],'''Can local visual and spacing rules produce V-like flight formations? NetLogo Flocking Vee Formations adapts flocking so birds approach visible neighbors, avoid obstructed views and excessive proximity, then match a nearby bird's speed and heading. The Info names Nathan and Barbosa's artificial-bird study as its loose source [1]. Empirical upwash research [2] and aerodynamic formation analysis [3] provide physical comparisons. The model uses a prescribed updraft distance but does not solve airflow or wingbeat energetics. V shapes and echelons illustrate coordination under limited vision and prioritized rules. Their appearance does not demonstrate measured energy savings, reproduce actual migratory behavior, or establish that a balanced formation will remain stable indefinitely.''')
add('slime-mold-network',[('jones','The particle-pattern approximation is explicitly cited in the model’s scientific lineage.'),('alim','Signal-propagation research provides biological context for adaptive transport-network behavior.'),('tero','Empirical biologically inspired network design offers a relevant comparison for efficiency and adaptation.')],'''How can distributed responses to nutrient signals produce a transport-like network? NetLogo Slime Mold Network approximates Physarum exploration through local movement and signal-dependent growth or withdrawal. Its Info describes biological tube flow as motivation, while the implemented approximation belongs to a reduced computational model. Particle-network research [1], signal-propagation studies [2], and adaptive-network experiments [3] frame what is illustrated. The simulation compresses cytoplasmic hydraulics, contractions, and material transport rather than resolving them directly. Network-looking patterns show how local rules can redistribute exploration toward resources. They do not establish that every visible strand is a physical tube, prove network optimality, or validate quantitative flow and growth predictions for an actual organism.''')
add('rock-paper-scissors',[('reichenbach','The model explicitly follows mobility-dependent biodiversity research in cyclic competition.'),('reichenbach2','The event-based spatial formulation is directly relevant to the model’s scientific lineage.'),('kerr','A bacterial experimental system supplies empirical context for local dispersal and cyclic competition.')],'''How does movement influence coexistence among cyclic competitors? NetLogo Rock Paper Scissors represents three local populations: red defeats green, green defeats blue, and blue defeats red. Reproduction, competition, and exchange of positions generate spatial patterns. The Info connects its event-based formulation to mobility studies [1,2], while bacterial competition experiments [3] provide an empirical comparison. The agents are abstract competitors rather than a calibrated microbial species assemblage; interaction symmetry and movement rates are declared assumptions. Spirals illustrate how local cyclic dominance can organize space and how mobility can change persistence. They do not prove that diversity is always stable, that more movement is always beneficial, or that identical rates describe real ecological interactions.''')
add('fairy-circles',[('tarnita','The Info explicitly identifies this multi-scale vegetation-pattern theory as the model’s inspiration.'),('juergens','Provides termite-based empirical interpretation relevant to the colony mechanism.'),('getzin','A spatial vegetation study offers an alternative perspective and tests on pattern interpretation.')],'''Can interacting processes create regular vegetation patterns at several scales? NetLogo Fairy Circles is inspired by a theory combining competition among termite colonies with vegetation feedback. The Info explicitly cites Tarnita and colleagues [1]; termite evidence [2] and spatial vegetation research [3] contextualize the mechanism and competing explanations. The simulation represents simplified colonies, resource effects, and patterned grass rather than a site-calibrated desert ecosystem. It illustrates how distinct local processes can interact to produce organized spatial structure. Rings and regular spacing are not sufficient to identify the true cause of every observed fairy-circle landscape. The model therefore supports comparison of assumptions and patterns, while field measurements and independent tests remain necessary for causal attribution.''')
add('plant-growth',[('thornley','A quantitative root–shoot allocation model provides a scientific comparison for sharing resources between functional compartments.'),('prusinkiewicz','A scientific monograph on algorithmic plant form contextualizes local growth rules and their architectural consequences.'),('hodge','Research on root plasticity in heterogeneous nutrients grounds the ecological question of resource-directed development.')],'''How can a growing structure concentrate effort where resources are available? NetLogo Plant Growth creates leaf-like and root-like cells that collect different resources but require both to survive. Directionally biased exchange of sugar and water helps sustain productive branches while unproductive regions can fail. Root–shoot allocation theory [1], algorithmic plant architecture [2], and root-plasticity research [3] provide scientific comparisons. The Info explicitly says the model is not intended to be biologically realistic. Its resource flows and cell addition rules are exploratory abstractions rather than plant vascular physiology. Adaptive-looking growth illustrates consequences of these rules; it does not establish optimal allocation, realistic developmental signaling, or a quantitative prediction of yield in a particular environment.''')
add('algae',[('konopka','Studies buoyancy regulation and vertical migration, directly matching the model’s light–nutrient tradeoff.'),('walsby','Reviews gas-vesicle biology and buoyancy mechanisms, providing context for the simplified density variable.'),('huisman','Examines mixing and light competition, exposing an important environmental driver beyond this simplified column.')],'''How can an organism balance light near the surface against nutrients deeper in water? NetLogo Algae represents a vertical aquatic column where algae change density as light-driven storage and metabolism alter their buoyancy. Research on vertical migration [1], buoyancy structures [2], and competition under mixing [3] provides scientific context. The Info traces the model to an Allan Konopka workshop prototype. Its density bookkeeping, light distribution, and resource rules are simplified, and the model does not resolve lake hydrodynamics or full cellular physiology. Repeated movement illustrates one possible response to opposing resource gradients. It does not establish a calibrated migration cycle, explain all phytoplankton strategies, or predict a specific lake's bloom dynamics.''')
add('habitat-preference-behavior',[('fretwell','The ideal-free distribution framework directly contextualizes resource-dependent habitat choice.'),('rosenzweighab','A theory of habitat selection supplies the link between local choice and population distribution.'),('morris','Critically synthesizes habitat selection and its ecological assumptions, useful for interpreting simplified preferences.')],'''How do individual habitat choices change the distribution of a population? NetLogo Habitat Preference Behavior explores the relation between local conditions, agent movement, and the resulting occupancy pattern. Ideal-free distribution theory [1], habitat-selection theory [2], and a later ecological synthesis [3] provide the scientific framing. The served model makes behavioral response to habitat differences explicit rather than treating a population distribution as a predetermined map. Its movement and preference rules simplify information, competition, and the relationship between habitat quality and fitness. Spatial concentration illustrates a consequence of those rules, not direct evidence that animals possess perfect information or that occupancy measures habitat quality. Empirical interpretation requires checking the model's behavioral assumptions against the particular organisms and environment.''')
S['habitat-preference-behavior']={'refs':[('gunn','Original humidity-response experiments relate directly to the model’s chamber comparison and reduced activity in preferred conditions.'),('waloff','Analyzes the mechanisms retaining terrestrial isopods in damper zones, supporting the kinesis interpretation.'),('isopod','A specialist scientific monograph provides terrestrial-isopod biology and environmental-response context beyond the simplified chamber rules.')],'miniText':'''Can different rates of random movement produce an apparent habitat preference? NetLogo Habitat Preference Behavior recreates a two-chamber pillbug experiment with moist or dry conditions. Agents move randomly, but in their preferred environment they move only half the time. Original humidity-response experiments [1], mechanistic isopod research [2], and terrestrial-isopod biology [3] provide direct scientific context. The model therefore illustrates retention through changed activity rather than requiring agents to navigate toward a known destination. Its binary conditions and fixed activity probability simplify sensory responses, water balance, and species differences. Unequal chamber occupancy is an outcome of these rules, not evidence of conscious choice, a complete behavioral mechanism, or validation against measured pillbug movement.'''}
